From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=85smund=20Collin?= <aakjaergaard@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 13 Jul 2026 20:08:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] resonance_tester: derive test motion limits from the
 actual test sequence
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The temporary acceleration and velocity limits were computed from the
configured accel_per_hz, ignoring both the ACCEL_PER_HZ command
override and the sweeping acceleration. As a result:

- TEST_RESONANCES/SHAPER_CALIBRATE with ACCEL_PER_HZ higher than the
  configured value generated moves that exceeded the limits: e.g. with
  the default accel_per_hz 75 and ACCEL_PER_HZ=120, the test requires
  16200 mm/s^2 and 30 mm/s but the limits were set to 10135 mm/s^2 and
  19.75 mm/s. The velocity clamp applies on all kinematics, silently
  attenuating the excitation across the sweep.
- Sweeping tests (SWEEPING_PERIOD > 0) exceeded the limits even without
  overrides: the sweeping motion adds sweeping_accel to the segment
  acceleration and, more significantly, a large velocity component
  (~139 mm/s at the default sweeping_accel 400 and period 1.2 vs the
  19.75 mm/s limit), so the sweeping movement was velocity-clamped.

Compute the limits from the generated sequence itself (max segment
acceleration and integrated peak velocity plus the previous margins),
which covers all overrides and generators by construction. Also drop
the dead test_accel_per_hz plumbing in ResonanceTester._run_test (it
assigned an attribute nothing reads, on the wrong object, and would
have leaked the override into subsequent tests otherwise; the override
already flows through prepare_test), and record the effective
accel_per_hz instead of the configured default in the CSV metadata.

resonance_tester: fix final deceleration move after the test sequence

The deceleration-to-stop issued when the test sequence ends with a
nonzero velocity (which happens with sweeping tests; plain vibration
pulses always return to zero) had two problems:

- It used last_v2, which holds the velocity at the start of the last
  segment, not the final velocity: the loop updates last_v after
  computing last_v2. For a sweeping test ending at 8.7 mm/s the decel
  distance was computed from 28.2 mm/s - about 10x too long for the
  actual speed.
- The distance was unconditionally negative, i.e. opposite to the
  direction of motion whenever the final velocity was positive, while
  the in-loop direction-change decel gets its sign correctly from the
  signed segment acceleration.

Compute the distance from the actual final velocity, signed to continue
in the direction of motion: d = 0.5 * v * |v| / max_accel.

resonance_tester: input validation and minor hardening

- SHAPER_CALIBRATE AXIS validation used a substring check
  (axis.lower() not in "xy"), so AXIS=XY was accepted: it silently
  calibrated only the Y direction (TestAxis("xy") maps any non-"x"
  name to the Y vibration direction) while applying the result to both
  axes. Restrict to exactly "x" or "y".
- Require MEAS_TIME > 0 in MEASURE_AXES_NOISE.
- Show the active-fans warning in TEST_RESONANCES too, not only in
  SHAPER_CALIBRATE - fan vibrations pollute both measurements.
- Replace two bare except clauses with the intended exception types.
- Fix help-string typos (specifed, Simular).

Signed-off-by: Åsmund Collin <aakjaergaard@gmail.com>
